Rachel Elizabeth Dillon wrote:
> Jove wrote:
> > Doug Freyburger wrote:
>
> >>As a compromise for wizards, I rather like studded leather armor
> >>until dragon scale mail comes available.  It does not interfere with
> >>spellcasting (very little or none at all I don't recall right now) and
> >>it
> >>gives some AC bonus.  On the one hand mithril with its good AC
> >>boost and lots of interference, on the other hand S/DGSM with
> >>superior AC boost and no interference, in the middle studded
> >>leather as a temporary compromise until ?DSM comes available.
>
> >   Don't forget that body armor is just one of shirt, body, cloak, boots,
> > helm, gloves, and shield.  Enough accessory armor can easily make up for
> > the two points of AC difference between elven mithril and studded
> > leather armor.  (So can a single highly enchanted piece of accessory
> > armor.)

Sure, but all of them have the same decision method.  When
given the choice of one item in that category that is not cursed,
wear it.  When given the choice among several, wear the one
with the best compromise between AC points and spell interference.

For shields, with classes capable to twoweapon I tend to wear
one until reday to twoweapon then ditch it.  With spellcasters
I check very carefully between AC points and spell interference.
A blessed +3 small shield becomes a no brainer in the early
game but as other AC sources improve I'll eventually ditch any
shield my spellcaster wears.

> You can't "make up for" AC difference. Your AC can always be better. This
> logic makes sense if your target AC is 0 or even -10. My target AC
> is -100.

My target AC is around -40 when all of the pieces are in play.
Shirt, GDSM, robe, speed boats, dexterity gloves, brilliance
helm all +4/5 fooproofed is nice.

> >   You both seem to be over-focussed on a single type of a single form of
> > AC.  (Other forms include: divine protection, spell of protection, rings
> > of protection, etc.)

Having a couple dozen AC points from altar camping makes up
for lack of ring and shield for me.

> In a discussion about which body armor to choose, focusing on body armor
> seems, well, reasonable. :)

Because the principles map to all the other armor types as well.

> You're right that there are many other ways to
> get two or three points of AC. My counterpoint would be that these ways stack,
> and I am more likely to run into body armor first. It is good that you
> bring up the big picture, though (see below)...

I'm likely to run into mithril before returning to minetown from
mines end.  By then I'll also have some other armor.  It's in
minetown coming up that first cut for many of the armor items
appears.  Either my pet takes out the watch or my chaotic
character does.  Then I'll have studded leather body armor,
gloves, small shield.  From there on the goal is to replace one
item at a time with my view of the ideal armor.

> >   A big problem is that the "best" wizard body armor changes rapidly in
> > the very early game.  A wizard should probably wear the first non-cursed
> > body armor that doesn't cause burdening, whether that's a leather jacket
> > or bronze plate mail.   (That can bring up stashing strategies.)
>
> Agreed.

Going down into the mines, mithril gets worn temporarily.
Coming up through the mines, that's when the watch generously
leaves me studded leather armor and gloves in their wills.

> >   After that almost any non-cursed armor still needs to be tried on to
> > determine enchantment.  If you find +5 orcish ring mail, wear it.
>
> But what if you have +5 elven mithril and +5 studded leather? Then the
> debate goes back to "In the early game, are 3 points of AC worth a hit to
> spellcasting ability?" Of course this is a theoretical discussion and a
> character should use whatever the best thing they have is, but theoretical
> analysis can help players make more informed choices in this space.

And for me the answer tends to be the studded leather.
Especially once I have plenty of other armor items.

Same for comparing dexerity and power gloves - Go with the better
enchanted but be ready to improve the type that does not
interfere with spells.  Boots, same principle says wear the best
early ones but only improve speed.  Helmets, same principle
says wear the best early ones but only improve at least ESP.

> Of course, your bringing up the big picture does raise an interesting
> point, which is "How much is the difference between 6 and 3 AC worth
> as compared to the difference between -13 and -16?" At -13 I might very
> well stick with leather armor instead of mithril no matter how early in
> the game it was, unless I had no intent of casting any spells for quite
> a while. Then again, if my max HP were low enough...

The max HP bit is my best argument for levelling up.  And if
my spellcaster knows create monster then I don't care at
all about purchasing AC as I can get it better from altar
dancing.

> You're right that it's situational, of course. It's always situational.
> But there are also general principles that are very useful, and "always
> shoot for a better armor class" is one of my favorites.

Better AC number, better type of armor, lower spell interference.
Always a compromise among them until the best types of
equipment arrives in each slot.



